<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <GenerateDependencyFile>false</GenerateDependencyFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128;NU5100</NoWarn>
    <PackageId>TINS.Native.Desktop</PackageId>
    <Description>Meta-package bundling TINS.Native.win-x64, TINS.Native.linux-x64, TINS.Native.osx-x64, and TINS.Native.osx-arm64, so a multi-platform consumer (e.g. a cross-targeted test project) can pull in native FFTW/OpenBLAS assets for every desktop RID with a single reference instead of adding each RID package individually. A single-RID deployment should still reference just its own TINS.Native.&lt;rid&gt; package directly -- this carries no native content of its own.</Description>
    <PackageTags>Neuroscience, Native, FFTW, OpenBLAS, Desktop</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- $(Version) isn't usable here: MinVer computes it via a build target that runs
         *after* restore, but restore needs a concrete version right now to resolve these
         PackageReferences (chicken and egg).

         This has been through two earlier iterations:
         1. A floating version ("0.0.0-alpha.1.*"): has no publishable meaning in a
            nuspec, so `dotnet pack` collapsed it to whatever concrete sibling version
            resolved *at pack time* and baked THAT in as the minimum, ratcheting up on every
            repack. Once an older sibling version got pruned from a downstream feed, this
            risked a genuine hard restore failure (floor higher than what that feed had).
         2. An abstract, height-less floor ("0.0.0-alpha.1"): never ratchets, but a
            literal "0.0.0-alpha.1" build will never exist, so NU1603 ("dependency version
            not found, nearest match used") fired on *every single restore*, everywhere,
            permanently. Benign, but noisy enough in practice (reported from a real
            downstream consumer, AxoSync) that it's worth avoiding for a floor we can
            actually pin correctly.

         Settled on pinning to "0.0.0-alpha.1.14" specifically: the one height at which
         all four sibling packages have actually been built and sit in the local feed
         together. As a plain (non-floating, non-bracketed) version string this is still a
         minimum-inclusive bound (">= 0.0.0-alpha.1.14"), not an exact pin, so any later
         height still satisfies it once repacked; but a NuGet global-package-cache with an
         older cached copy (e.g. 1.12) no longer silently satisfies it either, forcing
         restore to reach for the real current version instead (this is what was actually
         happening: AxoSync's cache had stale 1.12/1.2 copies that satisfied the old
         "anything >= 0.0.0-alpha.1" floor, not a feed content problem).

         Trade-off, accepted deliberately: this floor needs bumping by hand (edit here, then
         repack) whenever the sibling packages move to a new height that should become the
         new baseline: back to needing a manual step, but only when actually intended,
         not silently on every incidental repack. Update this (and
         MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers in Directory.Build.props) together if that
         identifier ever changes (e.g. once real release tags start). -->
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.win-x64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1.14" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.linux-x64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1.14" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.osx-x64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1.14" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.osx-arm64" Version="0.0.0-alpha.1.14" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
